From 72787de0a6fb81da21ec130ac3bb449f6fb92ccb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kai Chen <tingkaic@nvidia.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Mar 2026 01:42:11 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Replace cp2112 sync xfer with async workqueue

Calling hid_hw_raw_request() and hid_hw_output_report() directly from
the I2C adapter or GPIO callback context blocks the calling thread until
the USB transfer completes or timeout after 5 sec
(USB_CTRL_SET_TIMEOUT). The long timeout can cause hung task panic when
the USB connection fails at the downstream I2C device initialization
stage.

Introduce an async workqueue for cp2112 to decrease the timeout
duration.
- An ordered workqueue executes the actual HID call while the caller
  waits on wait_for_completion_timeout().
- An atomic 'abandoned' flag arbitrates xfer ownership on timeout:
  - the work handler frees the xfer if the caller already gave up.
  - otherwise the caller frees it.
- Queue depth is capped at CP2112_XFER_QUEUE_MAX to provide -EBUSY
  backpressure.
- Timeout duration can be configured via DT property
  `hid-xfer-timeout-ms` with default value 500ms.

Test:
* Normal case: all i2c xfer (for downstream IOx) and gpio xfer (for irq
polling) pass without errors.

* Failure case: HID xfer timeout after 500ms when manually disable USB
xfers.

Steps:
1. Find the HID device name of cp2112.
```
root@vr-nvl-bmc:~# basename `dirname /sys/bus/hid/devices/0003\:0955\:CF11.000*/i2c-70`
0003:0955:CF11.0003
```

2. monitor the kernel log of the cp2112 device (on the other session or
background)
```
root@vr-nvl-bmc:~# grep '0003:0955:CF11.0003' /proc/kmsg &
```

3. Disable asynchronous & periodic schedule of USB host controller to
simulate the failure case.
```
root@vr-nvl-bmc:~# while true; do devmem 0x1e6a3020 32 0x10005; done
```

4. Since cp2112 driver polls the gpio state for interrupt usage (at
10Hz), the kernel log from step 2 should show the HID xfer timeout w/
~500ms gap.
```
<3>[ 5397.622237] cp2112 0003:0955:CF11.0003: error requesting GPIO values: -110
<3>[ 5398.242221] cp2112 0003:0955:CF11.0003: error requesting GPIO values: -110
<3>[ 5398.862278] cp2112 0003:0955:CF11.0003: error requesting GPIO values: -110
<3>[ 5399.482230] cp2112 0003:0955:CF11.0003: error requesting GPIO values: -110
<3>[ 5400.102243] cp2112 0003:0955:CF11.0003: error requesting GPIO values: -110
<3>[ 5400.722220] cp2112 0003:0955:CF11.0003: error requesting GPIO values: -110
<3>[ 5401.342225] cp2112 0003:0955:CF11.0003: error requesting GPIO values: -110
<3>[ 5401.962217] cp2112 0003:0955:CF11.0003: error requesting GPIO values: -110
```

5. Manually poll the downstream IOx pin value to simulate full workqueue
case.
```
root@vr-nvl-bmc:~# basename /sys/bus/i2c/devices/70-0050/gpiochip*
gpiochip20
root@vr-nvl-bmc:~# while true; do gpioget gpiochip20 0; done
gpioget: error reading GPIO values: Connection timed out
gpioget: error reading GPIO values: Connection timed out
gpioget: error reading GPIO values: Device or resource busy
gpioget: error reading GPIO values: Device or resource busy
gpioget: error reading GPIO values: Device or resource busy
```

